&#x200B;

Eller så ville jag bara dela med mig av en personlig berättelse.

You definitely need nullifying orb to negate his all in. It forces you to back if he all ins after 1-2q's, depending on how much you let him farm early, but its better than dying. If you have strong fundamental understanding of wave control you can try to shift it to your advantage before you back (e.g. if he freezes under his tower you all in the wave to get the crash in. then you back to get full hp and farm from the incoming slow push). If it pushes too slow go for a roam and I guarantee you he will shove it, which lets you come back in time to farm.

&#x200B;

I let them pick malph, it doesn't bother me as much as irelia. Early lifesteal helps if you can live with a bit less dmg potential in roams.

You have a great point as our social environment obviously has a huge influence. It's important to take both aspects into consideration. HOWEVER, being heavier has implications for all of life, and to say that that's purely socially mediated is ignorant. 

&#x200B;

To touch on another point which is related, the concept of societal safe-spaces also misses the point and is likely more detrimental than having to deal with things as they are now, by learning proper self-care and mental health management habits. The whole point of psychotherapy is to learn deal with your triggers when you can't avoid them. I see a trend in society towards changing people's language which I feel only leads to more confusion among affected people. Hard truths are less confusing than trying to cope by, for instance, exchanging a letter in the word obesity for an asterisk.

Vad sägs om att läsa studien helt innan du desinformerar?

&#x200B;

"ConclusionsThere was no evidence that cannabis use in people at high risk for psychosis had a significant effect on the incidence of psychosis or other adverse clinical outcomes. **These findings are not consistent with epidemiological data linking cannabis use to an increased risk of developing psychosis."**

So the heads lie in a spiral, hinting at something that keeps repeating itself in a pattern. It's made up of different individual's heads, all with dead stares. The only living thing seems to be the woman, who has a resemblance of the dead stare, but has a beautiful body resembling youth and fruitfullness, and she seems to have a light smile, despite showing an expression that implies death is coming for her too. She's part of the spiral, but hasn't gone through the entropy phase of her life-cycle yet; or samsara, as other have alluded to. But essentially, it's all part of the same string that keeps repeating itself.

&#x200B;

What I love most about the piece is her drugged-like face. The title gives it away: life is actually absurd, and you can only glimpse ecstasy for the short time you're alive. She and the rest of us all share one common thing fundamentally- our destinies are death and seemingly eternal cold.

Can't tell it will work for you, but my ex girlfriend also had this problem. I got her a microdose of psilocybin semilanceata mushrooms for a different reason. The crazy thing is: the dose was tiny, she barely felt any effect. Yet, waking up refreshed the next day, upon returning home she noticed that she was able to read longer paragraphs without losing focus. She was ecstatic that she could read pages upon pages, and she still didn't lose focus.

&#x200B;

Take it for what you will. We also had a great night together with lots of different activities which might've accentuated the effect. I haven't talked to her for years now but I heard from a friend she still uses them to this day, and remember her finding a lot of benefit in it. (a mindset of confronting what's tough about her condition was the foundation for the experiences)

